// The drawn iPod — the one asset that appears on every surface, so it is worth drawing properly.
//
// **Every dimension is derived from `body-height` and nothing else.** That is what makes the device
// unable to resize itself, which is the bug docs/GUI.md §6 exists to prevent, and it means one
// number takes it from a thumbnail to a hero.
//
// ── Where the numbers come from ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **Measured, from Rockbox's own scale drawing of this device** —
// `manual/rockbox_interface/images/ipodvideo-front.svg`, a vector front elevation shipped with the
// Rockbox manual. **The tests cannot read that file** — `resources/` is gitignored, so it is not
// there for anybody else — and an earlier version of this comment claimed they did. What the tests
// check instead is the thing the drawing was itself checked against: the hardware. See
// `the_drawn_ipod_is_the_shape_of_a_real_one`.
//
// The parse is self-checking: on a 104.1 mm body the extracted display comes out **50.7 × 38.2 mm**
// against the **50.8 × 38.1 mm** a 2.5-inch 4:3 panel must be — 0.2 %, which is how we know the
// right path was read and that it is the active area rather than the glass.
//
// **But 0.2 % is not small here, and the drawing does not get to decide the screen's size.**
// 0.4866 / 0.3672 is 1.32516 where 4:3 is 1.33333 — the drawing's own 0.61 % error, harmless on
// paper and fatal in the one rule this project will not bend, because a well that is not 4:3
// stretches a 320 × 240 buffer *in one axis only*. It shipped: at the 658 px body this file used to
// declare, the panel was drawn 320.18 × 241.62 with `image-fit: fill` — 1.00057 across and 1.00674
// down, non-uniform and non-integer, and under nearest-neighbour that duplicates about 1.6 rows out
// of 240 at fixed positions, which land on RetailOS's own 1 px separator rules.
//
// So the dependency is inverted, which is how MAME solves the same problem: **the drawing governs
// where the screen sits; the hardware governs how big it is.**
//
//   SCREEN_W = 50.8 / 104.1 = 0.48799
//   SCREEN_H = 38.1 / 104.1 = 0.36599      ratio exactly 4:3, by construction
//
// The cost is one number, below the drawing's own error: the symmetric inset moves by 0.00065 of
// body height — 0.43 px at hero, 0.07 mm on a real device — and the symmetry test still holds.
//
// The first version of this file used none of that. Its body and screen sizes were roughly right
// because they came from published specs, but the *placement* was arithmetic I chose to make the
// column add up — and it was wrong by 66 % on where the screen sits, 34 % on the centre button and
// 13 % on the wheel. It is very hard to see that a proportion is invented; it is easy to see that
// the result does not look like an iPod.
//
// ── The controls, added with the bench (docs/GUI.md §7.4) ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// **Every drawn control goes to the machine, always, and to nothing else.** With no machine the
// only one that does anything is the centre button, which powers on. MENU, Prev, Next, Play, the
// ring and the hold switch do nothing until there is a machine, and pressing one of them puts its
// sentence on the **cradle label** — held while the pointer is down, released on pointer-up. Not a
// timed message on the shelf: that is a toast, and principle 5 bans it by name.
//
// **The hit test is `wheel.rs`'s and is never re-derived here.** This file reports the pointer's
// offset from the wheel's centre and nothing else; `WheelRing::hit` decides what it means. The one
// number that has to be a *size* here — the centre button's target — is **pushed in** as
// `select-d` rather than computed from a ratio, because §7.4 is explicit that two rules for that
// region disagreed with each other and with the model, and the model's is the one that wins.

import { Ink, Metric, Motion } from "tokens.slint";
// Generated by build.rs from src/geometry.rs (docs/GUI.md §16.9). `@` resolves through the
// compiler's library paths only, so no file on disk can shadow it.
import { Geometry } from "@geometry";

// The four click-wheel marks. Vector paths, never font glyphs — docs/GUI.md §7, written after
// twelve glyphs shipped to the operator as empty squares.
component WheelMark inherits Path {
    in property <string> kind;
    in property <color> ink;
    fill: ink;
    viewbox-width: 12;
    viewbox-height: 8;
    commands: kind == "prev"
        ? "M 5 0 L 0 4 L 5 8 Z M 11 0 L 6 4 L 11 8 Z"
        : kind == "next"
        ? "M 0 0 L 5 4 L 0 8 Z M 6 0 L 11 4 L 6 8 Z"
        : "M 0 0 L 5 4 L 0 8 Z M 7 0 L 8.6 0 L 8.6 8 L 7 8 Z M 10 0 L 11.6 0 L 11.6 8 L 10 8 Z";
}

export component IPod inherits Rectangle {
    in property <length> body-height: 420px;
    in property <color> chassis: #2b2b2d;
    // Passed rather than computed: Slint has no luminance function, and the alternative is the
    // markup guessing at a colour's brightness with arithmetic that would be wrong for gold.
    in property <bool> dark-chassis: true;

    // §10.1: **an iPod that has not been decided yet**, which is what an empty bench has. The full
    // drawing in `Colour::Unspecified` `#E4E4E2` at `Geometry.ghost-opacity`. Not black — drawing
    // an unknown chassis black invents a fact about somebody's iPod.
    //
    // **It is a state of its own and is never derived from the chassis colour.** A real device
    // whose ROM did not state a colour is `#E4E4E2` too, and it is solid: `chassis` is what the
    // device is made of, `ghost` is whether there is a device. Deriving one from the other would
    // ghost every unspecified iPod in the library.
    //
    // What it dims is `shell` and not `glass` — see the body comment on `shell` for the
    // arithmetic, which is measured rather than argued.
    in property <bool> ghost: false;
    in property <image> screen-source;
    in property <bool> screen-lit: false;
    // The device is the control (§ the operator, 2026-08-21): pressing it is what starts it, so
    // there is no button in the chrome to press instead.
    in property <bool> pressable: false;

    // ── §7.4: is there a machine? ──
    //
    // The one flag that decides whether the wheel, the four labels and the hold switch mean
    // anything. It is **not** the same question as `pressable`: the centre button is live either
    // way — with a machine it is Select, without one it is power-on, which is what a button press
    // on a dead iPod does.
    in property <bool> has-machine: false;

    // §7.4: a first-class concept and not a button. Its own command (`holdsw on|off`), its own
    // field (`Stats::hold`), its own key. With no machine it sits in the off position, inert.
    in property <bool> hold: false;

    // ── §8.1 item 7: the depressed controls, and the 1.6 s that is real ──
    //
    // A press's *release* is held for `MIN_BUTTON_HOLD` ≈ 1.6 s of wall time at this window's
    // instruction rate, so a drawn button depresses when your finger moves and stays depressed
    // until `Stats::buttons` clears. That is a fact about the emulator worth being able to watch,
    // and it is a **read** of the machine rather than a local animation — which is why these are
    // pushed in one per button rather than decoded from a mask here: Slint has no bitwise
    // operators, so a mask in the markup would have to be re-encoded as arithmetic.
    //
    // With no machine `Stats::buttons` is 0 and stays 0, so Rust raises and drops these itself over
    // `tight`. §7.4 says both halves for exactly this reason.
    in property <bool> held-menu: false;
    in property <bool> held-next: false;
    in property <bool> held-prev: false;
    in property <bool> held-play: false;
    in property <bool> held-select: false;

    // §7.4: the centre button's hit region is `WheelRing::select` — `outer × 0.465`, which is 39 %
    // wider than the drawn disc. **The ratio lives in `wheel.rs` and must not be written here.**
    // Two rules for this region is precisely what §7.4 records as having disagreed with each other
    // and with the model, so Rust pushes the diameter in and the markup never re-derives it.
    //
    // The default is the drawing's own diameter, which is the honest fallback for `slint-viewer`
    // and for a build whose Rust has not pushed it yet: a **smaller** target, never a wrong one.
    in property <length> select-d: Geometry.centre-d * body-height;

    // §7.3: the drawn device is `AccessibleRole::Image` with the panel's own description, and
    // announces nothing about the program. The cradle is the Button; this is the picture.
    in property <string> panel-description;

    callback clicked();
    callback pressed-device();

    // The wheel's pointer stream, in the wheel's own coordinates: the offset from its centre, x
    // right and y **down**, which is what `wheel::position_at_angle` takes.
    callback wheel-down(length, length);
    callback wheel-moved(length, length);
    callback wheel-up();
    // §7.4: an inert control was pressed. The sentence goes on the cradle label and is held while
    // the pointer is down.
    callback inert(string);
    callback inert-released();
    // The hold switch, when there is a machine to own it.
    callback hold-toggled();

    property <length> h: body-height;
    property <color> marks: dark-chassis ? #76767a : #a9a9a6;
    // §7.2, §8.1 item 4: changing the device on the bench cross-dissolves `chassis` and the
    // wheel-markings colour over `tight` and **moves nothing** — `hero` is a pushed constant and is
    // never animated (§8.2 rule 1). Only reachable with no machine, because while there is one the
    // bench draws it and only it.
    animate marks { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }

    // §7.4's two sentences. They are the cradle's for the duration of the press, and they are
    // **pushed** rather than typed here.
    //
    // They were literals, and one of them was `machine::NO_MACHINE` written a second time — one
    // sentence in two files, which is the divergence every other caption in this program is
    // arranged to prevent. It stopped being merely untidy the day the bench could hold a machine:
    // *there is no machine* is false over a running iPod, and this file cannot tell the two states
    // apart. Rust can, so Rust says which sentence it is.
    in property <string> wheel-refusal;
    in property <string> hold-refusal;

    width: Geometry.body-aspect * h;
    height: h;

    // ── §10.1's ghost, and the one element it must not reach ────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Everything the device is made of lives in here — the moulded face, the wheel, the four marks,
    // the hold switch and every `TouchArea` — so that ONE `opacity` can dim the whole object at
    // once. **`glass` is deliberately outside it**, and that is measured rather than argued.
    //
    // Slint composites `opacity` over the whole subtree as one group, so the literal reading of
    // §10.1 — *the full drawing at 45 %* — would lift the glass from `#08080a` toward `bg-sunken`
    // and land it at **2.97 : 1** against the well, under the 3 : 1 bar
    // `the_cradle_colours_clear_three_to_one_against_the_well` holds everything on that surface to.
    // Undimmed it is 12.65 : 1. §10.1's own next sentence is *the glass is dark and completely
    // empty* and §12.2 gives `Off` a `#08080a` glass; one `opacity` cannot honour both sentences,
    // and the one with a number behind it wins. `the_ghost_dims_the_body_and_not_the_glass`
    // recomputes both figures from `ui/tokens.slint`'s palette rather than quoting these.
    //
    // Nothing moves and nothing is re-parented in the process: every child below is absolutely
    // positioned off `root.h` and `Geometry.*`, and `shell` is the full body rectangle. `opacity`
    // does not gate a `TouchArea`, so the hit test is untouched — a ghost is as pressable as a
    // device, which is exactly what §10.1 asks of the empty bench.
    shell := Rectangle {
        x: 0;
        y: 0;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        border-radius: Geometry.corner-r * h;

        // Glossy polycarbonate, not a flat fill. Two stops read as the real moulded face at every
        // size; a third starts to look like a button.
        background: @linear-gradient(180deg,
            chassis.brighter(dark-chassis ? 0.22 : 0.06) 0%,
            chassis 42%,
            chassis.darker(dark-chassis ? 0.10 : 0.05) 100%);

        // §8.1 item 4, amended: the device cross-dissolve is now THREE properties — `chassis`,
        // `marks` and this — and all three take `tight`. **Not `lively`**: §8.2 rule 2 confines
        // `ease-out-back` to drawer page depth, and an overshoot on an opacity ramping to 1.0 is a
        // value the compositor clamps, which is a curve lying about its own end.
        //
        // The Screen is supposed to be unbound for the dissolve's duration (§7.2) on a plain
        // `settling: bool` Rust raises for 140 ms — that flag is **not** declared here, because a
        // property nothing writes is the defect §20 item 15 names in a new place.
        opacity: root.ghost ? Geometry.ghost-opacity : 1.0;
        animate opacity { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
        animate background { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }

        // A hairline, not a drop shadow: nothing in this program floats (§2.4), and a shadow is the
        // visual grammar of floating.
        border-width: 1px;
        border-color: dark-chassis ? #00000060 : #0000001f;

        // ── The hold switch (§7.4) ──
        //
        // Drawn on the top edge, where it is on the hardware. Its geometry is `HOLD_W` × `HOLD_H`,
        // inset `HOLD_INSET` from the trailing edge — three ratios that have been in `geometry.rs`
        // since the drawing was measured and that nothing drew until now.
        hold-switch := Rectangle {
            x: parent.width - self.width - Geometry.hold-inset * h;
            y: 0;
            width: Geometry.hold-w * h;
            height: Geometry.hold-h * h;
            border-radius: self.height / 2;
            background: chassis.darker(dark-chassis ? 0.35 : 0.12);
            border-width: 1px;
            border-color: dark-chassis ? #00000080 : #00000024;

            // The marker the switch uncovers. On the hardware it is orange; here it is `warn`,
            // which is the same colour this program already uses for *you are about to be able to
            // do damage*.
            Rectangle {
                x: 0;
                y: 0;
                width: parent.width / 2;
                height: 100%;
                border-radius: parent.border-radius;
                background: Ink.warn;
                opacity: root.hold ? 1.0 : 0.0;
                animate opacity { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }

            // The nub. It slides; nothing else about the device moves.
            nub := Rectangle {
                x: root.hold ? parent.width - self.width : 0;
                y: 0;
                width: parent.width / 2;
                height: 100%;
                border-radius: parent.border-radius;
                background: @linear-gradient(180deg,
                    chassis.brighter(dark-chassis ? 0.26 : 0.08) 0%,
                    chassis 100%);
                border-width: 1px;
                border-color: dark-chassis ? #ffffff1a : #00000014;
                animate x { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }

            // §16.5: **never `enabled:` on a `TouchArea`.** A disabled one forcibly clears
            // `has_hover` and forwards the event to whatever is underneath, so a control that
            // carries its own reason is unbuildable that way. The gate is a plain boolean inside
            // the handler.
            hold-touch := TouchArea {
                pointer-event(ev) => {
                    if (ev.kind == PointerEventKind.down) {
                        if (root.has-machine) { root.hold-toggled(); }
                        else { root.inert(root.hold-refusal); }
                    }
                    if (ev.kind == PointerEventKind.up && !root.has-machine) {
                        root.inert-released();
                    }
                }
            }

            accessible-role: AccessibleRole.switch;
            accessible-label: "hold";
            accessible-checked: root.hold;
            accessible-enabled: root.has-machine;
            accessible-description: root.has-machine ? "" : root.hold-refusal;
        }

        // ── The click wheel ──
        wheel := Rectangle {
            x: (parent.width - self.width) / 2;
            y: Geometry.wheel-top * h;
            width: Geometry.wheel-d * h;
            height: Geometry.wheel-d * h;
            border-radius: self.width / 2;
            background: @linear-gradient(180deg,
                chassis.brighter(dark-chassis ? 0.10 : 0.02) 0%,
                chassis.darker(dark-chassis ? 0.04 : 0.03) 100%);
            border-width: 1px;
            border-color: dark-chassis ? #ffffff14 : #00000014;

            // The ring's own pointer stream. It reports **where**, never **what** — `wheel.rs`
            // owns the radii and the 96 detents, and a second hit test here is the disagreement
            // §7.4 records. The centre button's own area is declared after this one and therefore
            // sits on top of it, so a press inside `select-d` never reaches here.
            ring-touch := TouchArea {
                width: 100%;
                height: 100%;
                pointer-event(ev) => {
                    if (ev.kind == PointerEventKind.down) {
                        if (!root.pressable) { root.clicked(); return; }
                        if (root.has-machine) {
                            root.wheel-down(self.mouse-x - self.width / 2, self.mouse-y - self.height / 2);
                        } else {
                            root.inert(root.wheel-refusal);
                        }
                    }
                    if (ev.kind == PointerEventKind.up && root.pressable) {
                        if (root.has-machine) { root.wheel-up(); }
                        else { root.inert-released(); }
                    }
                }
                // Fires only while the pointer is down, which is exactly the drag a rotation is.
                moved => {
                    if (root.pressable && root.has-machine) {
                        root.wheel-moved(self.mouse-x - self.width / 2, self.mouse-y - self.height / 2);
                    }
                }
            }

            // MENU is a word on the real device, so it is a word here. The other three are marks.
            Text {
                y: Geometry.menu-top * h;
                width: 100%;
                text: "MENU";
                horizontal-alignment: center;
                font-size: Geometry.menu-size * h;
                font-weight: 600;
                letter-spacing: Geometry.menu-track * h;
                color: marks;
                opacity: root.held-menu ? 0.55 : 1.0;
                animate opacity { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }
            WheelMark {
                kind: "prev";
                ink: marks;
                x: Geometry.mark-inset * h;
                y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
                width: Geometry.mark-w * h;
                height: Geometry.mark-h * h;
                opacity: root.held-prev ? 0.55 : 1.0;
                animate opacity { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }
            WheelMark {
                kind: "next";
                ink: marks;
                x: parent.width - self.width - Geometry.mark-inset * h;
                y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
                width: Geometry.mark-w * h;
                height: Geometry.mark-h * h;
                opacity: root.held-next ? 0.55 : 1.0;
                animate opacity { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }
            WheelMark {
                kind: "play";
                ink: marks;
                x: (parent.width - self.width) / 2;
                y: parent.height - self.height - Geometry.mark-inset * h;
                width: Geometry.play-w * h;
                height: Geometry.mark-h * h;
                opacity: root.held-play ? 0.55 : 1.0;
                animate opacity { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }

            // The centre button — the one you press to start.
            //
            // **The material does not go here** (§6.5): a glossy blue disc is UI state painted on
            // the object, and a screenshot of it is no longer a picture of an iPod. The cradle
            // carries *press this* instead. What moves here is the moulding's own highlight, on
            // hover and on press, which is what a real disc does under a finger.
            centre := Rectangle {
                x: (parent.width - self.width) / 2;
                y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
                width: Geometry.centre-d * h;
                height: Geometry.centre-d * h;
                border-radius: self.width / 2;
                background: @linear-gradient(180deg,
                    chassis.brighter(dark-chassis
                        ? (centre-touch.has-hover && pressable ? 0.30 : 0.14)
                        : 0.03) 0%,
                    chassis 100%);
                border-width: 1px;
                border-color: dark-chassis ? #ffffff1a : #00000014;
                opacity: (centre-touch.pressed || root.held-select) ? 0.82 : 1.0;
                animate background, opacity { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }

            // The centre button's **target**, which is bigger than its drawing and is not the same
            // rectangle. Declared last so it is on top of `ring-touch`, and sized from the pushed
            // `select-d` rather than from `CENTRE_D` — see the property's own note.
            //
            // §16.5 again: always enabled, gated inside the handler, so `has-hover` stays honest
            // and the cradle can say why nothing happened.
            centre-touch := TouchArea {
                x: (parent.width - self.width) / 2;
                y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
                width: root.select-d;
                height: root.select-d;
                mouse-cursor: root.pressable ? MouseCursor.pointer : MouseCursor.default;
                clicked => {
                    if (!root.pressable) { root.clicked(); return; }
                    root.pressed-device();
                }
            }
        }

        // Anywhere else on the body focuses the fixture and does nothing else — which is what a
        // fixture should do. It is **not** *select this one*: §7.2 deletes device-switching from
        // the bench, so there is nothing else to select, and the carousel whose neighbours the
        // earlier version of this comment described was deleted with the chrome bar (§18.1).
        body-touch := TouchArea {
            z: -1;
            clicked => { root.clicked(); }
        }
    }

    // ── The screen ──
    //
    // The active area sits inside a black glass panel that overhangs it, which is what gives a real
    // iPod its bezel. Drawing the framebuffer flush to the body — the first version — is why it read
    // as a sticker rather than a display.
    //
    // The drawing insets the screen by the same 0.0525 h on the left, the top and the right, and
    // that symmetry is how the parse was confirmed to have found the right rectangle rather than
    // some other one. Since §6.6 took the screen's *size* off the drawing, the left inset is now an
    // output — (0.5917 − 0.48799) / 2 = 0.05186 — and differs from the top by 0.00065 of body
    // height, which is 0.43 px at hero and inside the test's 0.002 tolerance.
    glass := Rectangle {
        x: (parent.width - self.width) / 2;
        y: Geometry.screen-top * h - Geometry.bezel-ratio * h;
        width: Geometry.screen-w * h + 2 * Geometry.bezel-ratio * h;
        height: Geometry.screen-h * h + 2 * Geometry.bezel-ratio * h;
        background: Ink.glass;
        border-radius: Geometry.glass-corner-r * h;
        border-width: 1px;
        border-color: #ffffff14;

        // §7.3, §16.7: the panel is a picture and says only what is on it. The role is a literal —
        // a role behind a ternary is a role an assistive technology cannot rely on.
        accessible-role: AccessibleRole.image;
        accessible-label: "the iPod's screen";
        accessible-description: root.panel-description;

        Image {
            x: (parent.width - self.width) / 2;
            y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
            width: Geometry.screen-w * h;
            height: Geometry.screen-h * h;
            source: screen-source;
            // The whole point (§2.8): nearest-neighbour, never smoothed, and a 4:3 well so 320×240
            // is never stretched either.
            image-rendering: pixelated;
            image-fit: fill;
            // §8.1 item 6, the wake: the backlight comes up rather than a window opening. Opacity
            // only — nothing here is tweened between two geometries, because there is only one
            // geometry (§8.2 rule 1). 220 ms, which is a duration of its own rather than `gentle`
            // borrowed and mislabelled.
            //
            // **No `visible: screen-lit` beside it**, and that was a real defect rather than a
            // tidiness point: `visible: false` lowers to a `Clip` whose geometry `passes/visible.rs`
            // leaves unset, so the element is cut the frame the property flips and the 220 ms ramp
            // from 1.0 to 0.0 ran entirely inside a clipped subtree. The wake was unaffected —
            // visible turned on first — so only park, §8.1 item 6's *reversed*, never rendered. At
            // `opacity: 0` this Image takes no layout cell, carries no TouchArea of its own and
            // catches nothing.
            opacity: screen-lit ? 1.0 : 0.0;
            animate opacity { duration: Motion.wake; easing: ease-out-quad; }
        }
    }
}
